windows-conemu|ConEmu's own ANSI parser - no xenl because its margin wrap is immediate and bce because every erase fills with the live attribute. Every string here is one that ConEmu's CEAnsi really acts on AND that org.dbcli's native renderer models; the omissions are as deliberate as the additions and are listed at the bottom of this line's continuation in src/c/conemu/DESIGN.md (caps audit). Kept out and why: cbt and hts/tbc - there is no tab-stop state anywhere (ESC H is ignored) so CSI Z can do nothing; smir/rmir and the mir flag - IRM has no body upstream so CSI @ always inserts regardless; smam/rmam - DECAWM only records a column for ConEmu's own writer because the SetConsoleMode that would toggle it is commented out; smkx/rmkx - DECCKM changes input and this terminal's input comes from console records so the mode selects nothing; blink and invis - SGR 5/6/8 hold no state on either leg; rmpch and the SGR 10/11 arms of sgr - no alternate-font path; ncv - underline bold and reverse all coexist with colour; mc5i - no printer; flash - ESC g flashes the ConEmu window which a renderer must not do and leaving it out makes readers fall back to bel; initc and ccc - the OSC is consumed but the palette never changes; xenl - measured absent on both writer legs. Three rulings are about the input leg, which no output measurement can reach: kbs stays ^H because jline's own key leg decodes it through Curses.tputs into 0x08 and binds 0x08 and 0x7f to the same widget, and all three Windows entries in this jline write it that way; kf13 and up plus the shifted arrow names (kLFT kRIT kUP kDN kHOM kEND kPRV kNXT kIC kDC) stay out because that leg synthesises the bytes for a modified key itself with no caps consulted, terminfo can name only the Shift arm, no widget is bound to a shifted key, and there is no modifyFunctionKeys decoder for the Ctrl and Alt arms; rep is in because CSI b has a body in CEAnsi and a model in the native renderer, with xterm's canonical value, whose character must be passed to jline's tputs as an int because toInteger parses a Character as a String and throws. u6 and u7 are in because the answer leg is no longer something this host waits on: Render.cpp:1235 arms CSI 6n and RenderJni.cpp:465 flush_reports writes the reply back onto CONIN$ as key events, spelled ESC [ row ; col R one-based off the window, which is the shape CursorSupport.java:85-135 compiles u6 into a regex for (so u6 is a report pattern, not a string this host writes, and %i is what turns its one-based answer into the zero-based cursor jline hands back). The private spelling CSI ? 6 n stays refused on purpose (Render.cpp:1230), which is why only the plain form is named. u8 and u9 stay out though CSI c and CSI > c are both answered (Render.cpp:1220-1221, replies at RenderJni.cpp:397-401): nothing in jline reads user8 or user9 - CursorSupport is the only consumer of any user capability and it reads user6 and user7 - and a value for a capability no code asks about would be invented here, which is what this table's first sentence forbids. The input-leg citations are in src/c/conemu/DESIGN.md §4.
	am, bce, msgr,
	colors#256, cols#80, it#8, lines#24, pairs#64,
	bel=^G, bold=\E[1m, clear=\E[H\E[2J,
	cr=^M, cub=\E[%p1%dD, cub1=\E[D, cud=\E[%p1%dB, cud1=\E[B,
	cuf=\E[%p1%dC, cuf1=\E[C, cup=\E[%i%p1%d;%p2%dH,
	cuu=\E[%p1%dA, cuu1=\E[A,
	civis=\E[?25l, cnorm=\E[?25h, cvvis=\E[?25h,
	smcup=\E[?1049h, rmcup=\E[?1049l,
	rc=\E8, sc=\E7,
	il=\E[%p1%dL, il1=\E[L,
	dl=\E[%p1%dM, dl1=\E[M,
	ich=\E[%p1%d@, ich1=\E[@, dch=\E[%p1%dP, dch1=\E[P, ech=\E[%p1%dX, rep=%p1%c\E[%p2%{1}%-%db,
	el=\E[K, ed=\E[J, el1=\E[1K,
	home=\E[H, hpa=\E[%i%p1%dG, vpa=\E[%i%p1%dd,
	ind=^J, indn=\E[%p1%dS, rin=\E[%p1%dT, ri=\EM, nel=\EE, ht=^I,
